      <section id="when" class="alt">
        <div class="wrap" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Honest limits</p>
          <h2>When it sends, and when it can't.</h2>
          <p class="sub">Rewake has no always-on service. In Zed it runs inside Zed, in Claude Code inside Claude Code, and for the other previews the system's own timer starts it at the reset. (On Linux without systemd or <code>at</code>, such as WSL and containers, it runs its own process, but only while a resume is waiting.) This is exactly what that means.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="when">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="group"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="2">In Zed</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Zed open, you're in another thread</td><td class="yes">Sent on time. Open the thread to see the reply.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>The agent is replying when it's due</td><td class="yes">Sent right after the reply.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A permission prompt during a scheduled reply</td><td class="yes">It waits for you. Rewake never approves anything.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Zed closed, or the computer asleep</td><td class="no">Nothing is sent then.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Back more than 15 minutes late</td><td class="no">Not sent on its own: Rewake asks you. A repeat skips that time.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Zed restarted</td><td class="no">Open the thread once; its messages go from then on.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="group"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="2">Outside Zed (previews)</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude Code open at the reset</td><td class="yes">The same session continues.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude Code closed at the reset</td><td class="no">Not sent: the continue is offered when you reopen the session.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Codex closed at the reset</td><td class="yes">The thread continues when you next open it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A closed Claude Code, Copilot CLI, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, Qwen Code, OpenCode or Antigravity CLI session</td><td class="yes">The system's timer continues it at the reset. Nothing of Rewake's runs until then.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>That session is open again at the reset</td><td class="no">Nothing is written into it: Rewake tells you instead.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>The computer asleep or off at the reset</td><td class="no">Nothing is sent then. More than half an hour late, Rewake tells you instead of sending.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="never">
        <div class="wrap" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Trust</p>
          <h2>What Rewake will never do.</h2>
          <p class="sub">Rewake sends messages into your agent while you may be away, so these are the lines it keeps. The code is open, so each can be checked, and <a href={href("docs/privacy-and-security/")}>the privacy and security page</a> says how.</p>
          <ul class="never-grid">
            <li><h3>Approve anything for you</h3><p>A permission prompt during a resumed reply waits for you.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Add a permission-bypass flag</h3><p>A resumed session is never given more permission than it had. The one approval flag Rewake passes is Gemini CLI's own default mode, which never widens yours. In Zed, you choose whether threads that bypass permissions are resumed automatically.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Type into your terminal</h3><p>It works through each agent's own plugin, hooks or resume command, and in Zed through the connection between Zed and the agent.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Resume a limit that waiting won't fix</h3><p>Credit, billing and spending limits get a message, not a resume.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Send what you didn't choose</h3><p>Only the messages you scheduled, ones you approved, or the resume message you turned on.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Send anything about you anywhere</h3><p>It sends nothing about you over the network and never sees your credentials: sign-in stays with your agent. Its only network use is fetching an agent's own program when Zed hasn't. Logs hold no message text.</p></li>
            <li><h3>Be hard to remove</h3><p>One command, <code>npx @codizelabs/agent-rewake uninstall</code>, shows every change, asks once and takes Rewake out of every place it's set up in.</p></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="sub">What Rewake sends runs with your account while you may be away. Turn on automatic resume only for threads you'd let run unattended. <a href="https://github.com/codizelabs/agent-rewake/blob/main/SECURITY.md">The security policy</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

